text:
VAR (Vector AutoRegressive) is a multivariate1 linear time series model, designed to capture the joint dynamics2 of multiple time series through an **interconnected system*.
VARs treat each endogenous variable3 in the system as a function of lagged values of all endogenous variables by default; plus exogenous variables only when explicitly added.
VARs offer a simple and flexible alternative to traditional multiple equation models.


3.1.1 A closer look: joint dynamics, interconnected system and endogeneity
In a VAR the variables are not studied side by side: they form a single system in which each one both drives and is driven by the others.
The interconnection is neither a one-off cross-effect nor unidirectional, it is mutual. Each variable enters the other’s equation, so influence runs in both directions and it propagates forward indefinitely. Neither variable can be held exogenous on the left hand side: this reciprocal dependence is exactly what endogeneity means here.
Example:
To fix ideas, let inflation (\(\pi\)) and GDP each depend on both their own lag and the other’s lag. Since the true data-generating process is unknown, the relationship is left as a generic function:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\pi_t &= f(\pi_{t-1},\, gdp_{t-1}) \\
gdp_t &= f(gdp_{t-1},\, \pi_{t-1})
\end{aligned}
\]
Following a single cross-channel makes the mechanism visible and more intuitive. Take the \(gdp \to \pi\) link (own-lag effects run in parallel, in exactly the same way):
\[
gdp_{t-1} \;\rightarrow\; \pi_t \;\rightarrow\; gdp_{t+1} \;\rightarrow\; \pi_{t+2} \;\rightarrow\; gdp_{t+3} \;\rightarrow\; \cdots
\]
This single thread, though, understates the structure. Own lags run alongside the cross-links, and an own lag is itself a product of the other variable one period earlier: \(gdp_{t-1}\) already carries \(\pi_{t-2}\). Every value is therefore fed, at every period, by both variables at once, as shown in the animation below, where horizontal arrows are own lags (persistence); diagonal arrows are cross-lags (spillovers).
This self-sustaining feedback loop is what makes the system genuinely joint, and it is the aspect most often underestimated when first learning VAR.

{
  const GDP = 0, PI = 1;
  const periods = ["t\u22121", "t", "t+1", "t+2"];
  const N = periods.length;

  const colGDP = "#4c78a8", colPI = "#e45756";
  const faint = "#ececec", firedCol = "#f6c667", hotArrow = "#f0a202";
  const shockCol = "#f0a202";
  const coldFill = "#f5f5f5", coldStroke = "#cfcfcf";
  const colOf = r =&gt; (r === GDP ? colGDP : colPI);

  const width = 680, height = 300;
  const mL = 88, mR = 30;
  const dx = (width - mL - mR) / (N - 1);
  const legendY = 22, periodY = 58, yTop = 104, yBot = 224, captionY = 288;
  const R = 15;
  const x = i =&gt; mL + i * dx;
  const yOf = r =&gt; (r === GDP ? yTop : yBot);

  const frames = [
    {type:"shock", caption:"A shock hits GDP at t\u22121."},
    {type:"trans", col:0, caption:"It carries into GDP (own lag) and spills into \u03C0 (cross-lag) \u2014 both now move."},
    {type:"trans", col:1, caption:"Now each variable feeds both: all four channels fire at once."},
    {type:"trans", col:2, caption:"\u2026and again \u2014 the feedback loop sustains itself, forward without end."},
    {type:"hold",  caption:"Every value is fed by both variables \u2014 one joint, interconnected system."},
    {type:"hold",  caption:"Every value is fed by both variables \u2014 one joint, interconnected system."}
  ];
  const total = frames.length;

  function stateAt(f) {
    const hot = [Array(N).fill(false), Array(N).fill(false)];
    hot[GDP][0] = true;
    const fired = new Set();
    let current = [];
    for (let idx = 1; idx &lt;= f && idx &lt; total; idx++) {
      const fr = frames[idx];
      if (fr.type !== "trans") continue;
      const c = fr.col;
      const arrowsThis = [];
      for (let r = 0; r &lt; 2; r++) {
        if (hot[r][c]) {
          arrowsThis.push([c, r, c + 1, GDP]);
          arrowsThis.push([c, r, c + 1, PI]);
        }
      }
      hot[GDP][c + 1] = true; hot[PI][c + 1] = true;
      if (idx === f) current = arrowsThis;
      else arrowsThis.forEach(a =&gt; fired.add(a.join(",")));
    }
    return {hot, fired, current, caption: frames[f].caption};
  }

  const container = d3.create("div").style("font-family", "system-ui, sans-serif");
  const controls = container.append("div")
    .style("display", "flex").style("gap", "6px").style("align-items", "center")
    .style("margin-bottom", "6px");
  const mkBtn = (label) =&gt; controls.append("button").text(label)
    .style("padding", "3px 10px").style("font-size", "13px")
    .style("border", "1px solid #ccc").style("border-radius", "6px")
    .style("background", "#fff").style("cursor", "pointer");
  const playBtn = mkBtn("Pause");
  const resetBtn = mkBtn("Reset");
  const prevBtn = mkBtn("\u2039");
  const nextBtn = mkBtn("\u203A");
  const holder = container.append("div");

  let f = 0, playing = true;

  function drawArrow(svg, i0, r0, i1, r1, mode) {
    const x0 = x(i0), y0 = yOf(r0), x1 = x(i1), y1 = yOf(r1);
    const a = Math.atan2(y1 - y0, x1 - x0);
    const sx = x0 + R * Math.cos(a), sy = y0 + R * Math.sin(a);
    const ex = x1 - (R + 5) * Math.cos(a), ey = y1 - (R + 5) * Math.sin(a);
    const col = mode === "hot" ? hotArrow : mode === "fired" ? firedCol : faint;
    const w = mode === "hot" ? 3.5 : mode === "fired" ? 2.5 : 1.2;
    svg.append("line")
      .attr("x1", sx).attr("y1", sy).attr("x2", ex).attr("y2", ey)
      .attr("stroke", col).attr("stroke-width", w)
      .attr("opacity", mode === "faint" ? 0.6 : 1)
      .attr("marker-end", "url(#ah-" + mode + ")");
  }

  function update() {
    const st = stateAt(f);
    holder.selectAll("*").remove();
    const svg = holder.append("svg")
      .attr("viewBox", [0, 0, width, height])
      .attr("width", width)
      .attr("style", "max-width:100%;height:auto;");

    const defs = svg.append("defs");
    [["faint", faint], ["fired", firedCol], ["hot", hotArrow]].forEach(([id, c]) =&gt; {
      defs.append("marker").attr("id", "ah-" + id)
        .attr("viewBox", "0 0 10 10").attr("refX", 9).attr("refY", 5)
        .attr("markerWidth", 7).attr("markerHeight", 7).attr("orient", "auto")
        .append("path").attr("d", "M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z").attr("fill", c);
    });

    // row baselines + labels (pi bigger)
    [["GDP", GDP, 16], ["\u03C0", PI, 26]].forEach(([lab, r, fs]) =&gt; {
      svg.append("line").attr("x1", mL - 20).attr("x2", width - mR)
        .attr("y1", yOf(r)).attr("y2", yOf(r)).attr("stroke", "#f2f2f2");
      svg.append("text").attr("x", mL - 34).attr("y", yOf(r) + fs / 3)
        .attr("text-anchor", "end").attr("font-weight", "bold")
        .attr("font-size", fs).attr("fill", colOf(r)).text(lab);
    });

    periods.forEach((p, i) =&gt; {
      svg.append("text").attr("x", x(i)).attr("y", periodY)
        .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 12).attr("fill", "#999").text(p);
    });

    // faint full lattice
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; N - 1; i++) {
      drawArrow(svg, i, GDP, i + 1, GDP, "faint");
      drawArrow(svg, i, PI,  i + 1, PI,  "faint");
      drawArrow(svg, i, GDP, i + 1, PI,  "faint");
      drawArrow(svg, i, PI,  i + 1, GDP, "faint");
    }
    // already-fired
    st.fired.forEach(k =&gt; {
      const a = k.split(",").map(Number);
      drawArrow(svg, a[0], a[1], a[2], a[3], "fired");
    });
    // current transition: all arrows together
    st.current.forEach(a =&gt; drawArrow(svg, a[0], a[1], a[2], a[3], "hot"));

    // nodes
    for (let r = 0; r &lt; 2; r++) {
      for (let i = 0; i &lt; N; i++) {
        const isHot = st.hot[r][i];
        svg.append("circle").attr("cx", x(i)).attr("cy", yOf(r)).attr("r", R)
          .attr("fill", isHot ? colOf(r) : coldFill)
          .attr("stroke", isHot ? colOf(r) : coldStroke).attr("stroke-width", 2);
      }
    }

    // prominent animated shock at frame 0
    if (frames[f].type === "shock") {
      const cx = x(0), cy = yOf(GDP);
      for (let idx = 0; idx &lt; 3; idx++) {
        const ring = svg.append("circle")
          .attr("cx", cx).attr("cy", cy).attr("r", R)
          .attr("fill", "none").attr("stroke", shockCol).attr("stroke-width", 3);
        ring.append("animate").attr("attributeName", "r")
          .attr("values", R + ";" + (R + 34)).attr("dur", "1.3s")
          .attr("begin", (idx * 0.43) + "s").attr("repeatCount", "indefinite");
        ring.append("animate").attr("attributeName", "opacity")
          .attr("values", "0.95;0").attr("dur", "1.3s")
          .attr("begin", (idx * 0.43) + "s").attr("repeatCount", "indefinite");
      }
      svg.append("circle").attr("cx", cx).attr("cy", cy).attr("r", R + 3)
        .attr("fill", colGDP).attr("stroke", shockCol).attr("stroke-width", 3);
      svg.append("text").attr("x", cx).attr("y", cy - R - 14)
        .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 15)
        .attr("font-weight", "bold").attr("fill", shockCol).text("SHOCK!");
    }

    // legend (raised)
    const lx = width - mR - 250, ly = legendY;
    svg.append("line").attr("x1", lx).attr("y1", ly).attr("x2", lx + 24).attr("y2", ly)
      .attr("stroke", "#bbb").attr("stroke-width", 2).attr("marker-end", "url(#ah-faint)");
    svg.append("text").attr("x", lx + 30).attr("y", ly + 4).attr("font-size", 11).attr("fill", "#666").text("own lag");
    svg.append("line").attr("x1", lx + 92).attr("y1", ly - 6).attr("x2", lx + 116).attr("y2", ly + 6)
      .attr("stroke", "#bbb").attr("stroke-width", 2).attr("marker-end", "url(#ah-faint)");
    svg.append("text").attr("x", lx + 122).attr("y", ly + 4).attr("font-size", 11).attr("fill", "#666").text("cross-lag");
    svg.append("circle").attr("cx", lx + 196).attr("cy", ly).attr("r", 5)
      .attr("fill", "none").attr("stroke", shockCol).attr("stroke-width", 2);
    svg.append("text").attr("x", lx + 206).attr("y", ly + 4).attr("font-size", 11).attr("fill", "#666").text("shock");

    // caption + counter
    svg.append("text").attr("x", width / 2).attr("y", captionY)
      .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 13).attr("fill", "#333").text(st.caption);
    svg.append("text").attr("x", width - mR).attr("y", captionY)
      .attr("text-anchor", "end").attr("font-size", 11).attr("fill", "#bbb")
      .text((f + 1) + "/" + total);
  }

  playBtn.on("click", () =&gt; { playing = !playing; playBtn.text(playing ? "Pause" : "Play"); });
  resetBtn.on("click", () =&gt; { f = 0; update(); });
  prevBtn.on("click", () =&gt; { playing = false; playBtn.text("Play"); f = (f - 1 + total) % total; update(); });
  nextBtn.on("click", () =&gt; { playing = false; playBtn.text("Play"); f = (f + 1) % total; update(); });

  const timer = setInterval(() =&gt; { if (playing) { f = (f + 1) % total; update(); } }, 1300);
  invalidation.then(() =&gt; clearInterval(timer));

  update();
  return container.node();
}

4.2 Part A - The scalar case: AR(1)

4.2.1 Recursive substitution
Starting with a first-order autoregressive process (to keep it simple the constant is dropped, it would only have shifted the mean), with \(w_t\) a white-noise shock:
\[
y_t = \phi\, y_{t-1} + w_t,
\qquad w_t \sim \text{WN}(0,\sigma^2)
\tag{4.1}\]
The idea is to rewrite \(y_t\) in another form, by substituting the process repeatedly into itself. The model holds at every date, so \(y_{t-1}\) can itself be written in terms of \(y_{t-2}\), and \(y_{t-2}\) in terms of \(y_{t-3}\), and so on:
\[
y_{t-1} = \phi\, y_{t-2} + w_{t-1}, \qquad
y_{t-2} = \phi\, y_{t-3} + w_{t-2}, \qquad \dots
\]
Each substitution moves one period further back: a new shock drops out and the power of \(\phi\) rises by one, step after step, until the chain reaches the initial value \(y_{-1}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
y_t &= \phi\, y_{t-1} + w_t \\[2pt]
    &= \phi\,\underbrace{(\phi\, y_{t-2} + w_{t-1})}_{y_{t-1}} + w_t
       = \phi^{2} y_{t-2} + \phi\,w_{t-1} + w_t \\[2pt]
    &= \phi^{2}\underbrace{(\phi\, y_{t-3} + w_{t-2})}_{y_{t-2}} + \phi\,w_{t-1} + w_t
       = \phi^{3} y_{t-3} + \phi^{2}w_{t-2} + \phi\,w_{t-1} + w_t \\[2pt]
    &\;\;\vdots \\[2pt]
    &= \phi^{\,t+1} y_{-1} + \phi^{\,t}w_0 + \cdots + \phi\,w_{t-1} + w_t
\end{aligned}
\tag{4.2}\]
It is shown also in an intercative way here below:

{
  const P = "\u03C6", E = "w", MIN = "\u2212", CD = "\u22EF";
  const M = 3;                 // concrete substitution steps (s = 0..M)
  const FINAL = M + 1;
  const total = M + 3;         // two hold frames on the final state

  const sub = s =&gt; "&lt;sub&gt;" + s + "&lt;/sub&gt;";
  const sup = s =&gt; "&lt;sup&gt;" + s + "&lt;/sup&gt;";
  const idx = n =&gt; n === 0 ? "t" : "t" + MIN + n;
  const phi = p =&gt; p === 0 ? "" : p === 1 ? P + "\u2009" : P + sup(p) + "\u2009";
  const yT  = lab =&gt; "y" + sub(lab);
  const eT  = lab =&gt; E + sub(lab);

  function resultHTML(s) {
    const lead = "&lt;span class='sbx-lead'&gt;" + phi(s + 1) + yT(idx(s + 1)) + "&lt;/span&gt;";
    let tail = "";
    for (let i = s; i &gt;= 0; i--) {
      const term = phi(i) + eT(idx(i));
      const cls = i === s ? "sbx-flash" : "sbx-old";
      tail += (i === s ? "" : " + ") + "&lt;span class='" + cls + "'&gt;" + term + "&lt;/span&gt;";
    }
    return "y" + sub("t") + " = " + lead + " + " + tail;
  }

  function finalHTML() {
    const lead = "&lt;span class='sbx-lead'&gt;" + P + sup("t+1") + "\u2009y" + sub(MIN + "1") + "&lt;/span&gt;";
    const tail =
      "&lt;span class='sbx-old'&gt;" + P + sup("t") + "\u2009" + eT("0") + "&lt;/span&gt; + " +
      "&lt;span class='sbx-old'&gt;" + CD + "&lt;/span&gt; + " +
      "&lt;span class='sbx-old'&gt;" + P + "\u2009" + eT(idx(1)) + "&lt;/span&gt; + " +
      "&lt;span class='sbx-old'&gt;" + eT("t") + "&lt;/span&gt;";
    return "y" + sub("t") + " = " + lead + " + " + tail;
  }

  function ruleHTML(s) {
    if (s &gt;= FINAL) return "&hellip; keep going until the index reaches " + MIN + "1.";
    return "Same equation, one period earlier: &nbsp; " +
      yT(idx(s + 1)) + " = " + P + "\u2009" + yT(idx(s + 2)) + " + " + eT(idx(s + 1));
  }

  const captions = [];
  captions[0] = "Start from the model: " + yT("t") + " depends on " + yT(idx(1)) + " and the shock " + eT("t") + ".";
  for (let s = 1; s &lt;= M; s++)
    captions[s] = "Reuse the identical equation one step back: one more shock drops out, the power of " + P + " grows by one.";
  captions[FINAL] = "After t+1 substitutions the chain reaches " + yT(MIN + "1") + ": a transient plus every past shock.";

  const ticks = [MIN+"1", CD, "t"+MIN+"4", "t"+MIN+"3", "t"+MIN+"2", "t"+MIN+"1", "t"];
  const nT = ticks.length;
  const tw = 600, th = 76, xL = 44, xR = tw - 24, baseY = 44;
  const tx = i =&gt; xL + (xR - xL) * i / (nT - 1);
  // index along the OLD ordering (0 = t, 1 = t-1, ...) mapped to the flipped axis
  const pos = k =&gt; nT - 1 - k;                 // k=0 (t) -&gt; rightmost, growing k -&gt; left
  const pointerTick = s =&gt; s &gt;= FINAL ? 0 : pos(s + 1);
  const shockTicks = s =&gt; {
    const set = new Set();
    if (s &gt;= FINAL) [0,1,2,3,4].forEach(k =&gt; set.add(pos(k)));
    else for (let k = 0; k &lt;= s; k++) set.add(pos(k));
    return set;
  };

  const wrap = d3.create("div").style("font-family", "system-ui, sans-serif");
  wrap.append("style").html(
    ".sbx-eq{font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:22px;margin:10px 0 4px;line-height:1.6;}" +
    ".sbx-eq sub{font-size:.7em;}.sbx-eq sup{font-size:.7em;}" +
    ".sbx-lead{background:#fff3d1;border:1px solid #f0a202;border-radius:5px;padding:1px 5px;}" +
    ".sbx-flash{color:#e45756;font-weight:bold;}.sbx-old{color:#333;}" +
    ".sbx-rule{font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:15px;color:#666;min-height:20px;}" +
    ".sbx-rule sub{font-size:.72em;}.sbx-cap{font-size:13px;color:#333;margin-top:4px;}"
  );

  const ctr = wrap.append("div").style("display","flex").style("gap","6px").style("margin-bottom","4px");
  const mk = t =&gt; ctr.append("button").text(t)
    .style("padding","3px 10px").style("font-size","13px").style("border","1px solid #ccc")
    .style("border-radius","6px").style("background","#fff").style("cursor","pointer");
  const bPlay = mk("Pause"), bReset = mk("Reset"), bPrev = mk("\u2039"), bNext = mk("\u203A");

  const ruleDiv = wrap.append("div").attr("class","sbx-rule");
  const eqDiv   = wrap.append("div").attr("class","sbx-eq");
  const tlHolder = wrap.append("div");
  const capDiv  = wrap.append("div").attr("class","sbx-cap");

  let f = 0, playing = true;

  function drawTimeline(s) {
    tlHolder.selectAll("*").remove();
    const svg = tlHolder.append("svg").attr("viewBox",[0,0,tw,th]).attr("width",tw)
      .attr("style","max-width:100%;height:auto;");
    svg.append("line").attr("x1",xL-10).attr("x2",xR+10).attr("y1",baseY).attr("y2",baseY).attr("stroke","#ddd");
    const sh = shockTicks(s), ptr = pointerTick(s);
    svg.append("text").attr("x",xL-16).attr("y",baseY-12).attr("font-size",12).attr("fill","#e45756").text(E);
    ticks.forEach((lab,i) =&gt; {
      svg.append("line").attr("x1",tx(i)).attr("x2",tx(i)).attr("y1",baseY-4).attr("y2",baseY+4).attr("stroke","#bbb");
      svg.append("text").attr("x",tx(i)).attr("y",baseY+20).attr("text-anchor","middle").attr("font-size",12).attr("fill","#888").text(lab);
      if (sh.has(i)) svg.append("circle").attr("cx",tx(i)).attr("cy",baseY-14).attr("r",5).attr("fill","#e45756").attr("opacity",0.85);
    });
    svg.append("circle").attr("cx",tx(ptr)).attr("cy",baseY).attr("r",11).attr("fill","#4c78a8").attr("stroke","#2f5c8f").attr("stroke-width",2);
    svg.append("text").attr("x",tx(ptr)).attr("y",baseY+4).attr("text-anchor","middle").attr("font-size",11).attr("fill","#fff").attr("font-weight","bold").text("y");
  }

  function update() {
    ruleDiv.html(ruleHTML(f));
    eqDiv.html(f &gt;= FINAL ? finalHTML() : resultHTML(f));
    capDiv.html(captions[Math.min(f, FINAL)]);
    drawTimeline(f);
  }

  bPlay.on("click", () =&gt; { playing = !playing; bPlay.text(playing ? "Pause" : "Play"); });
  bReset.on("click", () =&gt; { f = 0; update(); });
  bPrev.on("click", () =&gt; { playing = false; bPlay.text("Play"); f = (f - 1 + total) % total; update(); });
  bNext.on("click", () =&gt; { playing = false; bPlay.text("Play"); f = (f + 1) % total; update(); });

  const timer = setInterval(() =&gt; { if (playing) { f = (f + 1) % total; update(); } }, 1500);
  invalidation.then(() =&gt; clearInterval(timer));

  update();
  return wrap.node();
}






Collecting the terms gives the general solution:
\[
y_t = \phi^{\,t+1} y_{-1} + \sum_{i=0}^{t} \phi^{\,i}\, w_{t-i}
= \phi^{\,t+1} y_{-1} + w_t + \phi\, w_{t-1} + \cdots + \phi^{\,t} w_0
\tag{4.3}\]
The value of \(y_t\) splits into two pieces: the initial condition \(\phi^{\,t+1} y_{-1}\), and a weighted sum of every shock since the start, with weight \(\phi^{\,i}\) on the shock \(i\) periods ago.


4.2.2 The dynamic multiplier and impulse response function
Since Equation 4.3 writes \(y_t\) as a linear function of the shocks, the effect of a single shock can be read off directly. This is the key result obtained by rewriting the process through recursive substitution.
Restarting the count at an arbitrary date \(t\), following the same logic applied above:
\[
y_{t+j} = \phi^{\,j+1} y_{t-1} + \sum_{i=0}^{j}\phi^{\,i}\, w_{t+j-i}
\tag{4.4}\]
and differentiating with respect to the shock \(w_t\):
\[
\frac{\partial\, y_{t+j}}{\partial\, w_t} = \phi^{\,j}
\tag{4.5}\]
This defines the dynamic multiplier: the response of \(y\), \(j\) periods ahead, to a one-unit shock today. Two features matter:

It depends only on the horizon \(j\), not on the date \(t\): the system reacts to a shock in the same way no matter when it lands.
This is exactly an impulse-response function, tracing \(\phi^{\,j}\) for \(j = 0, 1, 2, \dots\) draws the system’s response to an impulse.

The shape depends entirely on \(\phi\):

\(0&lt;\phi&lt;1\): monotone geometric decay toward zero;
\(-1&lt;\phi&lt;0\): decay with alternating sign (damped oscillation);
\(\phi&gt;1\): explosive monotone growth;
\(\phi&lt;-1\): explosive oscillation.

The dividing line is \(|\phi|&lt;1\): inside it a shock’s effect eventually dies out and the system is stable; outside it the effect grows without bound, the process is explosive. The borderline \(\phi=1\) is the random walk, where \(\phi^{\,j}=1\) for every \(j\) and a shock’s effect is permanent.
Move the slider through all four regimes — this is the response \(\partial y_{t+j}/\partial w_t = \phi^{\,j}\):

viewof phi = Inputs.range([-1.2, 1.2], {value: 0.8, step: 0.05, label: "\u03C6"})







{
  const H = 20;
  const data = d3.range(0, H + 1).map(j =&gt; ({j, resp: Math.pow(phi, j)}));
  return Plot.plot({
    height: 300,
    marginLeft: 60,
    x: { label: "horizon j \u2192" },
    y: { label: "\u2191 response to a unit shock", grid: true },
    marks: [
      Plot.ruleY([0]),
      Plot.barY(data, { x: "j", y: "resp",
        fill: d =&gt; d.resp &gt;= 0 ? "#4c78a8" : "#e45756" })
    ]
  });
}








4.2.3 From recursive substitution to the Wold representation
Now let the process have been running not from date \(-1\) but from the infinite past, so, let \(t\to\infty\) in Equation 4.3. When \(|\phi|&lt;1\) the term \(\phi^{\,t+1} y_{-1}\to 0\) vanishes, and only the shock sum survives:
\[
y_t = \sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\phi^{\,i}\, w_{t-i}
= w_t + \phi\, w_{t-1} + \phi^{2} w_{t-2} + \cdots
\tag{4.6}\]
This is the MA(\(\infty\)), or Wold representation. Where the current value \(y_t\) is written as a weighted sum of all past shocks. It is nothing more than recursive substitution pushed to its limit under stationarity, and its weights \(\phi^{\,i}\) are exactly the dynamic multipliers of Equation 4.5.
The same object is reached algebraically with the lag operator \(L\) (with \(L\,w_t = w_{t-1}\)): writing Equation 4.1 as \((1-\phi L)\,y_t = w_t\) and inverting,1
\[
y_t = \frac{w_t}{1-\phi L}
= \Big(\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\phi^{\,i} L^{i}\Big) w_t,
\tag{4.7}\]
which reproduces Equation 4.6 term by term. The geometric series converges for exactly the reason that \(|\phi|&lt;1\).



4.2.4 For curious mind: from AR(1) to AR(2), AR(3),…, AR(n)
So far the Wold representation has been obtained by recursive substitution, and its weights turned out to be the powers \(\phi^{\,i}\). That result is specific to the AR(1). This section introduces the tool that handles any number of lags — the lag operator — and shows what changes, and what does not, when moving to an AR(\(p\)).

4.2.4.1 The weights are the impulse response, whatever the model
Write the Wold representation with generic weights \(\psi_i\):
\[
y_t = \psi_0 w_t + \psi_1 w_{t-1} + \psi_2 w_{t-2} + \cdots
= \sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\psi_i\, w_{t-i}
\tag{4.8}\]
Shifting Equation 4.8 forward by \(j\) periods gives \(y_{t+j}\) as a weighted sum of shocks up to date \(t+j\). Only one term in that sum contains the shock \(w_t\) — the one with \(t+j-i=t\), that is \(i=j\) — and it equals \(\psi_j w_t\). Differentiating,
\[
\frac{\partial\, y_{t+j}}{\partial\, w_t} = \psi_j
\tag{4.9}\]
This holds for any autoregressive order: the dynamic multiplier at horizon \(j\), the Wold weight \(\psi_j\), and the impulse response at horizon \(j\) are three names for the same number. For the AR(1), \(\psi_j = \phi^{\,j}\) and Equation 4.9 reduces to Equation 4.5.
The whole problem therefore collapses into a single question: what are the \(\psi_i\)?


4.2.4.2 The lag operator
The lag operator \(L\) is an instruction, not a number: it shifts a series back one period.
\[
L\,y_t = y_{t-1}, \qquad L^{2} y_t = y_{t-2}, \qquad L^{k} y_t = y_{t-k}
\tag{4.10}\]
Applied to the AR(1), substituting \(y_{t-1}=L y_t\) and collecting \(y_t\):
\[
y_t - \phi L\, y_t = w_t
\;\Longrightarrow\;
(1-\phi L)\, y_t = w_t
\tag{4.11}\]
The bracket is the model itself, rewritten with everything on the left. Call it \(\phi(L) = 1-\phi L\).
The Wold representation can be written the same way. Replacing each lagged shock by \(w_{t-i}=L^{i}w_t\) and collecting \(w_t\):
\[
y_t = \left(1 + \phi L + \phi^{2} L^{2} + \phi^{3} L^{3} + \cdots\right) w_t
\tag{4.12}\]
Call this second polynomial \(\psi(L)\). Substituting Equation 4.12 into Equation 4.11, the equality must hold for any sequence of shocks, so the two polynomials multiply to one:
\[
(1-\phi L)\left(1 + \phi L + \phi^{2} L^{2} + \cdots\right) = 1
\tag{4.13}\]
The product is immediate to verify: multiplying by \(1\) reproduces the series, multiplying by \(-\phi L\) reproduces it shifted up one degree and with opposite sign, and the two cancel term by term except for the leading \(1\).
So \(\psi(L)\) is the inverse of \(\phi(L)\), in exactly the sense in which \(1/5\) is the inverse of \(5\): the polynomial that multiplied by \(\phi(L)\) gives one. The model can then be “divided through”:
\[
y_t = \frac{1}{\phi(L)}\, w_t = \psi(L)\, w_t
\tag{4.14}\]
The two polynomials are the two directions of the same road. \(\phi(L)\) strips the dynamics out of the series and leaves the shock — it subtracts from \(y_t\) precisely the part that was predictable from the past. \(\psi(L)\) runs the other way, rebuilding the series from its shocks. The AR form describes \(y_t\) through its own past; the MA form describes it through the shocks. Same process, two readings — and the second is the one in which the impulse response is written in plain sight.


4.2.4.3 AR(p): the weights follow a recursion
Recursive substitution still works with more lags, but it becomes unmanageable: with two lags there are two \(y\) terms to chase at every step, with three lags three. The polynomial method, on the other hand, is indifferent to the order.
For an AR(\(p\)),
\[
\phi(L) = 1-\phi_1 L - \phi_2 L^{2} - \cdots - \phi_p L^{p},
\qquad \phi(L)\, y_t = w_t
\tag{4.15}\]
The inverse \(\psi(L)=\psi_0+\psi_1 L+\psi_2 L^{2}+\cdots\) is no longer known in advance, so its coefficients are recovered one at a time by imposing \(\phi(L)\psi(L)=1\). Since the constant polynomial \(1\) has zero coefficient on every power of \(L\), matching coefficients degree by degree gives
\[
\psi_0 = 1, \qquad
\psi_i = \phi_1\psi_{i-1} + \phi_2\psi_{i-2} + \cdots + \phi_p\psi_{i-p}
\tag{4.16}\]
with the convention \(\psi_j=0\) for \(j&lt;0\). For an AR(2), explicitly:
\[
\psi_1 = \phi_1, \qquad
\psi_2 = \phi_1^{2}+\phi_2, \qquad
\psi_3 = \phi_1^{3}+2\phi_1\phi_2
\tag{4.17}\]
The interpretation is transparent: \(\psi_2\) collects the two routes by which a shock two periods old reaches today — through the first lag twice (\(\phi_1^2\)), or through the second lag directly (\(\phi_2\)).
The AR(1) is the degenerate case of Equation 4.16. With only \(\phi_1\) present the recursion becomes \(\psi_i = \phi\,\psi_{i-1}\): every weight is the previous one times the same factor, which iterated from \(\psi_0=1\) gives the pure powers \(\psi_i=\phi^{\,i}\). With two or more lags each weight mixes the previous ones, and the closed form disappears.
One consequence worth noting: distant lags do not affect early responses. In an AR(3), \(\psi_1\) and \(\psi_2\) are identical to those of an AR(2), because \(\phi_3\) only enters from \(\psi_3\) onward — a third lag cannot influence the response before three periods have passed.


4.2.4.4 Why more lags matter: the shape of the response
Since the impulse response is the sequence \(\psi_0,\psi_1,\psi_2,\dots\), changing the recursion changes the shape of the response itself. Two numerical examples make the point.
A hump. An AR(2) with \(\phi_1=0.6\), \(\phi_2=0.3\) gives
\[
1,\;\; 0.60,\;\; 0.66,\;\; 0.576,\;\; 0.544,\;\dots
\]
The response at horizon 2 is higher than at horizon 1. An AR(1) with the same \(\phi_1=0.6\) would give \(1,\,0.60,\,0.36,\,0.216,\dots\) — monotonically decaying, as \(\phi^{\,i}\) always must. A hump-shaped response, where the peak effect arrives with delay rather than on impact, is simply not available with a single lag. It is, however, exactly what macroeconomic data display: a monetary policy shock reaches its maximum effect on inflation several quarters later, not immediately.
A damped cycle. An AR(2) with \(\phi_1=0.6\), \(\phi_2=-0.5\) gives
\[
1,\;\; 0.60,\;\; -0.14,\;\; -0.384,\;\; -0.160,\;\; 0.096,\;\; 0.138,\;\dots
\]
The response stays positive for a while, turns negative for a while, then positive again, fading as it goes — a genuine wave with a period of roughly six. This is different from the sign-flipping of an AR(1) with \(\phi&lt;0\), where the sign alternates at every step. Damped cycles arise when the roots of \(\phi(z)=0\) are complex, and they are the mechanism through which a linear model generates business-cycle dynamics.









